    "result_text": "The build is green. The full test suite (the `AcceptanceTest`, which is the only test class) passes, including the new `coreFormatAndGlobalSequence` checkpoint that verifies the `customerCode` format, prefix, and global sequence.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `customerCode` assignment following standard Spring conventions:\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `customerCode` field (`@Column(name = \"customer_code\")`) with getter/setter, alongside the existing derived fields.\n\n2. **`service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`** \u2014 assigned the code in `saveOwner(...)` for new owners only (`owner.isNew() && customerCode == null`, so updates never re-assign). A private `generateCustomerCode(lastName)` builds `<LAST3>-<NNNN>`:\n   - `LAST3` = upper-cased first three letters of `lastName` (length-safe substring),\n   - `NNNN` = `String.format(\"%04d\", ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1)` \u2014 one more than the current global owner count, zero-padded to 4 digits.\n\n   The count runs inside the same transaction; JPA's auto-flush ensures a just-created owner is included when the next owner's sequence is computed, so consecutive creates differ by exactly 1 (as the test asserts).\n\n3. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `customerCode` as a `readOnly` string property on the `Owner` schema (with the `SMI-0007` example). The generated `OwnerDto` gains the field, and MapStruct auto-maps it from the entity in `toOwnerDto`, so it's returned by `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n4. **`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `customer_code` column to the `owners` table (schema is initialized from these SQL files; tests use hsqldb).\n\nThe change satisfies the spec: e.g. an owner with `lastName` \"smithers\" created when there are 6 owners gets `SMI-0007`, and the next owner \"jones\" gets `JON-0008`.",
